      "body": "Hi,\r\n\r\nI am using Pop!_OS 20.04\r\n```\r\n03:05:52 15 ❯  lsb_release  -a\r\nNo LSB modules are available.\r\nDistributor ID: Pop\r\nDescription:    Pop!_OS 20.04 LTS\r\nRelease:        20.04\r\nCodename:       focal\r\n```\r\n\r\nkernel version is 5.4.0\r\n\r\n```\r\n03:08:54 15 ❯  uname -a\r\nLinux pop-os 5.4.0-7626-generic #30~1588169883~20.04~bbe668a-Ubuntu SMP Wed Apr 29 21:00:02 UTC  x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\nI installed bpftrace by apt.\r\n\r\n```\r\nsudo apt install bpftrace\r\n```\r\n\r\nBut tcpconnect.bt prints weird destination port.\r\n\r\n\r\nAt a terminal, run curl to get google.com:80\r\n\r\nAnd at another terminal, run tcpconnect.bt\r\n```\r\n\r\n03:04:10 15 ❯  /usr/bin/bpftrace /sbin/tcpconnect.bt\r\nAttaching 2 probes...\r\nTracing tcp connections. Hit Ctrl-C to end.\r\nTIME     PID      COMM             SADDR                                   SPORT  DADDR                                   DPORT\r\n15:04:13 980915   curl             192.168.0.9                             60490  172.217.175.14                          13429\r\n^C\r\n```\r\n\r\nDPORT shows 13429, not 80.\r\n\r\n\r\nbpftrace depends on LLVM 9.\r\nMaybe is it a reason of this problem?\r\n\r\n```\r\n03:04:15 15 ❯  apt depends bpftrace\r\nbpftrace\r\n  Depends: libbpfcc (>= 0.12.0)\r\n  Depends: libc6 (>= 2.27)\r\n  Depends: libclang1-9 (>= 1:9~svn359771-1~)\r\n  Depends: libgcc-s1 (>= 3.0)\r\n  Depends: libllvm9 (>= 1:9~svn298832-1~)\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\nINSTALL.md suggests to use LLVM 7.\r\n\r\nSo I built bpftrace from source with LLVM 7. And tcpconnect.bt works as expected. yay\r\n\r\n```\r\n03:11:44 15 ❯  ldd /home/esrse/.local/bin/bpftrace\r\n        linux-vdso.so.1 (0x00007ffd2757f000)\r\n        libbfd-2.34-system.so => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libbfd-2.34-system.so (0x00007f661a382000)\r\n        libopcodes-2.34-system.so => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libopcodes-2.34-system.so (0x00007f661a224000)\r\n        libbcc.so.0 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libbcc.so.0 (0x00007f6616878000)\r\n        libclang-7.so.1 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libclang-7.so.1 (0x00007f661502c000)\r\n        libLLVM-7.so.1 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libLLVM-7.so.1 (0x00007f6611769000)\r\n        libstdc++.so.6 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libstdc++.so.6 (0x00007f6611588000)\r\n        libgcc_s.so.1 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libgcc_s.so.1 (0x00007f661156b000)\r\n        libc.so.6 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6 (0x00007f6611379000)\r\n        libz.so.1 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libz.so.1 (0x00007f661135d000)\r\n        libdl.so.2 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libdl.so.2 (0x00007f6611357000)\r\n        libelf.so.1 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libelf.so.1 (0x00007f661133b000)\r\n        libtinfo.so.6 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libtinfo.so.6 (0x00007f661130b000)\r\n        libpthread.so.0 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libpthread.so.0 (0x00007f66112e6000)\r\n        libm.so.6 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libm.so.6 (0x00007f6611197000)\r\n        /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 (0x00007f661a639000)\r\n        libffi.so.7 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libffi.so.7 (0x00007f661118b000)\r\n        libedit.so.2 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libedit.so.2 (0x00007f6611153000)\r\n        librt.so.1 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/librt.so.1 (0x00007f6611148000)\r\n        libbsd.so.0 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libbsd.so.0 (0x00007f661112c000)\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\nBut if I build bpftrace from source with LLVM 9, the build process succeeds but tcpconnect.bt prints weird destination port again. oops\r\n\r\n```\r\n03:19:09 15 ❯  ldd /home/esrse/.local/bin/bpftrace\r\n        linux-vdso.so.1 (0x00007ffe8de3c000)\r\n        libbfd-2.34-system.so => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libbfd-2.34-system.so (0x00007f8628173000)\r\n        libopcodes-2.34-system.so => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libopcodes-2.34-system.so (0x00007f8628015000)\r\n        libbcc.so.0 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libbcc.so.0 (0x00007f8624669000)\r\n        libclang-9.so.1 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libclang-9.so.1 (0x00007f862294b000)\r\n        libLLVM-9.so.1 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libLLVM-9.so.1 (0x00007f861e6fc000)\r\n        libstdc++.so.6 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libstdc++.so.6 (0x00007f861e51b000)\r\n        libgcc_s.so.1 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libgcc_s.so.1 (0x00007f861e4fe000)\r\n        libc.so.6 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6 (0x00007f861e30c000)\r\n        libz.so.1 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libz.so.1 (0x00007f861e2f0000)\r\n        libdl.so.2 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libdl.so.2 (0x00007f861e2ea000)\r\n        libelf.so.1 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libelf.so.1 (0x00007f861e2ce000)\r\n        libtinfo.so.6 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libtinfo.so.6 (0x00007f861e29e000)\r\n        libpthread.so.0 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libpthread.so.0 (0x00007f861e279000)\r\n        libm.so.6 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libm.so.6 (0x00007f861e12a000)\r\n        /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 (0x00007f862842b000)\r\n        libffi.so.7 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libffi.so.7 (0x00007f861e11e000)\r\n        libedit.so.2 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libedit.so.2 (0x00007f861e0e6000)\r\n        librt.so.1 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/librt.so.1 (0x00007f861e0db000)\r\n        libbsd.so.0 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libbsd.so.0 (0x00007f861e0bf000)\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWhen I modify tcpconnect.bt as shown below, destination port column prints correct number.\r\n\r\n```\r\nkprobe:tcp_connect\r\n{\r\n  $sk = ((struct sock *) arg0);\r\n  $inet_family = $sk->__sk_common.skc_family;\r\n\r\n  if ($inet_family == AF_INET || $inet_family == AF_INET6) {\r\n    if ($inet_family == AF_INET) {\r\n      $daddr = ntop($sk->__sk_common.skc_daddr);\r\n      $saddr = ntop($sk->__sk_common.skc_rcv_saddr);\r\n    } else {\r\n      $daddr = ntop($sk->__sk_common.skc_v6_daddr.in6_u.u6_addr8);\r\n      $saddr = ntop($sk->__sk_common.skc_v6_rcv_saddr.in6_u.u6_addr8);\r\n    }\r\n    $lport = $sk->__sk_common.skc_num;\r\n    $dport = $sk->__sk_common.skc_dport;\r\n    $last_var = $sk->__sk_common.skc_dport;  // this line is added\r\n\r\n    // Destination port is big endian, it must be flipped\r\n    $dport = ($dport >> 8) | (($dport << 8) & 0x00FF00);\r\n\r\n    time(\"%H:%M:%S \");\r\n    printf(\"%-8d %-16s \", pid, comm);\r\n    printf(\"%-39s %-6d %-39s %-6d\\n\", $saddr, $lport, $daddr, $dport);\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhy does this happen?\r\n\r\nI hope to dig into it more but I do not have enough background of BPF, so I gave up.\r\n\r\nCould you have a look at this problem and find a reason?\r\n\r\nAnyway, now I am OK with bpftrace built with LLVM 7.\r\n",
